hi I'm Matt wells for recent TV I'm here  in Las Vegas at Freedom Fest and I'm  joined by michelle bernard school choice  reform advocate and hidden journalist  welcome tell us a little bit how you got  involved in the kind of school reform  movement sure absolutely education has  been an issue that's been important to  me my entire life but in particular a  few years ago I was you know like many  Americans sitting home watching  television the nation was celebrating  the fiftieth anniversary of Brown versus  Board of Education and and there was a  lot obviously to celebrate what I was  thinking you know isn't this interesting  we're celebrating the 50th anniversary  of Brown versus Board of Education I  live in the Washington Metropolitan Area  and many of the same arguments that my  parents had when I was a child and that  many parents have as to the inefficiency  of the DC public school system we were  still having 50 years later and I kept  thinking to myself what would Thurgood  Marshall be thinking if he could look at  the state of our nation's K through 12  education system it's not just from the  perspective of African American children  but all children particularly poor  children you know our schools are just  as separate and just as on people as  they were when he fought the Brown  versus Board of Education battle it's  not so much race-based now what we see  today is that education is that the  quality of your education is based on  your zip code and if you don't have a  great zip code your chance of really  realizing the America  dream is very very small and that has  what is what propelled me to really  dedicate most of my time and energy to  education so what specifically are you  advocating pushing and changing what do  you try to well personally I'm a real  big advocate personal choice I believe  school choice is the next evolution of  Brown versus Board of Education I think  that any parent regardless of zip code  or grandparent child caregiver concerned  citizen should be able to take their tax  dollars and put their child in the  school that they think best meets their  child's needs that beings that I believe  in comprehensive education reform from A  to Z one of the things that I am most  heavily focused on is really trying to  develop grassroots boots on the ground  civil rights moment of our time of  parents children grandparents concerned  citizens all across the nation that will  really march on Washington March on the  steps of their state legislators and  demand educational opportunity and  equality for all children in this  country today you know I I reflect back  on when I was a kid and I was studying  history and you look at the old history  books of the black and white photos from  that from that time period of Brown  versus Board of Education and ending  segregation in the United States and  that was a big civil rights movement not  a movement in our nation's history and  to me the education reform battle of  today is an extension of that movement  and that's what I'm working on that's  what I'm creating you know we've got the  academics and the policymakers and the  people who look at education reform from  that level I'm the person who really  wants to take it to a different level  and really meet with parents and meet  with  you know one of the things that that I  like to do is for example go into you  know low-income housing developments and  talk to parents and say you know what is  it that you want for your child and  every parent that I've ever met has  always said I want them to have an  excellent education and I don't want  them to have to live here and so what I  want to do is work with those people to  explain to them what do we mean when we  talk about school choice or parental  choice what do we mean by education  reform and also explain to parents that  they have the tools within themselves to  demand change from the government we are  seem to be living in a moment now where  there's a lot more traction for these  ideas Waiting for Superman a lot more  kind of bipartisan yeah opportunity what  do you foresee happening in the next  let's say five years or so well you know  I think that Waiting for Superman in the  cartel documentary called the boys of  Muraki have done a lot to make the  notion of education reform a part of pop  culture so for people who are very  wealthy live in zip codes where your  houses cost more and you have a really  good education and that you might assume  that all children have access to the  kind of kids schools that their kids do  it has its brought it it's brought at  home and so I think what we're going to  be able to revert to them I'm hoping we  will see in the next five years is a  broader coalition of people who are  dedicating themselves to school choice  into comprehensive education reform my  personal perspective is that when you go  out and you talk to and you know school  choice reformers by and large the people  who are really really engaged are white  they're males they are Christian they  are conservative you have Democrats and  left the center activists who as we sort  of make a joke and say that they're sort  of in the closet because they feel that  they're going to get beaten up by their  party or by their colleagues if they  speak out and say I'm for school  appearance for school reform I'm for  school choice I am Who I am you know  definitely in on the side of the  of the American population that believes  that we need to do something about the  power the teachers unions have on our  education system and so one of the  things I'm thinking we're going to see  in the next five years to see a broader  coalition that includes more people that  are Democrats more people that are left  the center and is more racially diverse  as well well thank you very much for a  recent TV I met well
 

